    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>Finding a van defect is not the problem. No record, or no proof you acted, is.</p><p>Under the Road Traffic Act 1988, a defect in a folder with no closure does not protect you.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>A defect is not a failure. A defect with no record, or no proof you acted, is.</li><li>Each report needs who found it, when, what it was and what was done.</li><li>A report with no follow-up is the weak point.</li><li>A photo removes doubt about how bad it is.</li><li>Recording the decision to keep using a van matters as much as the defect.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The law",
      "heading": "Telling someone is not a system",
      "html": "<p>Under the Road Traffic Act 1988, using a motor vehicle in a dangerous state on a public road is an offence. The driver must not move a vehicle they know is unroadworthy. The operator must not allow it.</p><p>DVSA expects a written way to report defects. Phoning the depot manager leaves no proof the defect was known, checked or fixed.</p><p>Traffic Commissioners look at how defects are managed. Defects with no follow-up look like a failure of management, not a slip. The same framework applies to van operators with no operator's licence.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The fields",
      "heading": "Every report needs the same things",
      "html": "<p>There is no single set format. These are the parts DVSA and Traffic Commissioner guidance keep returning to.</p><p><strong>Registration.</strong> Which vehicle.</p><p><strong>Date and time.</strong> When it was found.</p><p><strong>Driver's name.</strong> Who found it.</p><p><strong>Description.</strong> Clear. \"Tyre issue\" is not enough. \"Near-side rear tyre: tread 2mm, small cut in sidewall\" is.</p><p><strong>Place on the vehicle.</strong> Which corner, panel or part.</p><p><strong>How bad.</strong> Is it safe to use? If not, it stops.</p><p><strong>Photo.</strong> Not required, but strongly advised.</p><p><strong>Action taken.</strong> Repaired, stood down or watched. Record it separately and link it back.</p><p><strong>Sign-off.</strong> Who said it could go back on the road, and when.</p><p>The photo is the part most often skipped. A photo of a cracked mirror before driving off is proof it was minor.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The template",
      "heading": "What goes on the form",
      "html": "<p>Use this on paper, or copy it into a digital system. Keep a pad in each vehicle.</p><p><strong>Van Defect Report. Driver completes:</strong> vehicle registration; date of check; time of check; driver's name; defect description; place on vehicle; photo taken (Yes / No); vehicle safe to use (Yes / No, and if No, do not move the vehicle); reported to; time reported; driver signature.</p><p><strong>Fleet manager or operator completes:</strong> action taken; repair done by; date back in service; manager sign-off.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Where paper fails",
      "heading": "Follow-up is the part that breaks",
      "html": "<p>The driver fills in the form. It goes to the fleet manager. It lands in a pile. The repair happens. The form is never updated.</p><p>Months later, DVSA asks. You hold a defect with no closure. It looks like you knew and did nothing.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Taking a van off the road",
      "heading": "When the van cannot be used",
      "html": "<p>Some defects are clear. A tyre with a bulge does not move. A cracked windscreen in the driver's sightline does not move. Faulty brakes are not a judgement call.</p><p>Others need a decision. A small oil seep is a defect, but may not stop the van.</p><p>Record any decision to keep using a defective vehicle: who made it, why, and the stop-gap. For example: \"booked for repair by end of week; driver told not to load beyond 500kg until fixed.\" A decision with no record looks the same as ignoring the defect.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>Must I record van defects in the UK?</h3><p>Yes. Under the Road Traffic Act 1988 and operator guidance, operators need a way for drivers to report defects, and proof the defects were checked and acted on. A defect reported only by phone gives no protection.</p><h3>Can a van be driven with a recorded defect?</h3><p>It depends. Some defects do not stop use, like a small cosmetic dent. Others mean the vehicle does not move: a tyre below the legal minimum, a cracked windscreen in the driver's sightline, a faulty brake light. The operator and driver share the call. Record the decision.</p><h3>What should a van defect report include?</h3><p>Registration, date and time found, the driver's name, a description, the place on the vehicle, a photo if you can, whether the van was taken off the road, and the date and type of repair. Keep the repair sign-off separate, but linked.</p><h3>How long must defect records be kept?</h3><p>DVSA guidance says at least 15 months for goods vehicles. For light vehicle operators with no operator's licence, at least 12 months is wise. Digital records cost nothing to keep and are quick to search.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/van-check-app-uk.html\">Van check app for UK fleets</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/fleet-vehicle-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Fleet vehicle inspection checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/dvsa-walkaround-check-free-template-uk.html\">DVSA walkaround check template</a></li></ul>"
    }
